The check is `if (length > 0)`, but the code reads `chunk[0]` (1 byte) and then `mng_get_long(&chunk[1])` which reads 4 more bytes — `chunk[1]`, `chunk[2]`, `chunk[3]`, `chunk[4]`. So the chunk must be at least 5 bytes long. If length is 1, 2, 3, or 4, this reads past the end of the heap-allocated `chunk` buffer (heap buffer overflow / out-of-bounds read).

The fix upstream (this is actually CVE-2017-... in ImageMagick, and GraphicsMagick has similar): the check should be `if (length >= 5)`.

Also note: `loop_level=chunk[0]` — chunk[0] can be up to 255, and `mng_info->loop_active[256]`... Let me check the size of loop_active array. If loop_level is a long and chunk[0] is a byte (0-255), then mng_info->loop_active[loop_level] — if the array is 256 entries, that's fine. Let me check.

But the main bug per the description: "the mng_LOOP chunk is not validated to be at least 5 bytes long". So a LOOP chunk with length 1 will cause an OOB read of 4 bytes past the allocated buffer.

Now, the chunk buffer is allocated via MagickAllocateMemory(length) — exactly `length` bytes. So with length=1, reading chunk[1..4] reads 4 bytes past a 1-byte allocation. With ASan, this is a heap-buffer-overflow (read). 

